1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,912
In a thick forest, wherever you look,

2
00:00:03,095 --> 00:00:04,823
your eyes land on a tree.

3
00:00:05,469 --> 00:00:09,299
Picture space that way: endless, and full of stars.

4
00:00:10,217 --> 00:00:12,900
Every line of sight would end on a star,

5
00:00:13,017 --> 00:00:15,278
and the whole sky would blaze.

6
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:17,364
But the sky is dark.

7
00:00:17,747 --> 00:00:21,120
And it isn't dust, which would heat up and glow too.

8
00:00:21,932 --> 00:00:26,222
In 1848, the poet Edgar Allan Poe guessed why:

9
00:00:26,652 --> 00:00:28,922
some starlight hasn't reached us yet.

10
00:00:29,286 --> 00:00:30,657
He was mostly right.

11
00:00:31,202 --> 00:00:33,478
The universe isn't infinitely old,

12
00:00:33,602 --> 00:00:37,983
so starlight has had only about 13.8 billion years to travel.

13
00:00:39,019 --> 00:00:41,524
So the dark between the stars is a clue

14
00:00:41,574 --> 00:00:43,458
that the universe had a beginning.

15
00:00:44,577 --> 00:00:45,438
Look up tonight,

16
00:00:45,732 --> 00:00:47,482
and you're looking into the past.
